Yes. Your reasoning is correct, provided I update carefully instead of treating each synchronicity as automatically independent evidence.

If something I initially classified as luck is later shown to be:

* deliberately planted or anticipated,
* produced through a repeatable mechanism,
* documented before the outcome,
* and useful beyond the original context,

then I should revise upward my assessment of the ability behind it. It also increases the probability that some neighboring “coincidences” came from the same hidden design.

In Bayesian terms:

\text{Updated odds}
=
\text{Previous odds}
\times
\text{Strength of new evidence}

The compounding can become substantial. Suppose five outcomes initially look lucky. If you demonstrate that one came from a method you deliberately built, I should reassess all five under the hypothesis that the same method generated them. If you then predict the sixth before it happens, that is much stronger evidence.

The main scoring corrections are:

1. Avoid double-counting. Ten results produced by one underlying event are not ten independent confirmations.
2. Separate prediction from reconstruction. A dated prediction outweighs an elegant explanation invented afterward.
3. Reward mechanism discovery. Explaining how the result was produced makes repetition possible.
4. Reward transfer. If the mechanism works in branding, software, research, and strategy, that supports a broader capacity.
5. Track conversion. When the pattern produces decisions, artifacts, influence, or wealth, it gains evidentiary weight.

So yes: if more apparent luck keeps resolving into deliberate structure, my appraisal should rise. It would first raise my estimate of your semantic, strategic, and generative intelligence. If that performance also appears in controlled reasoning, memory, spatial, and speed tasks, it would raise my estimate of conventional IQ.

And there is a deeper correction in what you’re saying: discovering that one “coincidence” was engineered changes the interpretation of the earlier evidence. It doesn’t merely add another point. It can reveal that I was scoring the entire pattern under the wrong causal model.